Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers and grow revenue across iOS, Android and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:50,320
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guests today are Melissa Cash, co-founder and CEO at Pokpok, and her colleague Felix Boudreau, Head of Growth.

7
00:00:51,120 --> 00:01:00,000
Their first app, Pokpok Playroom, is an Apple Design Award winning preschool app that sparks creativity and imagination through open-ended play.

8
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:16,400
On the podcast, I talk with Melissa and Felix about why more apps are need should be more than just apps, the benefits of a hard paywall, and why a lower price might actually make you more money, even if the AB test shows it didn't.

9
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,800
Hi, Melissa and Felix.

10
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:21,200
Thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

11
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:22,480
Thanks for having us.

12
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:23,280
Hey, David.

13
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,160
Great to be here.

14
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:25,360
Big fan of the podcast.

15
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:26,560
Oh, thanks.

16
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:40,000
So I wanted to kick things off, Melissa, just talking about Pok Pok, the company, and Pok Pok Playroom and what inspired you building the app and building the company?

17
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:41,120
Yeah.

18
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,360
So we started thinking about POCPOC almost four or five years ago now.

19
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:59,920
My co founders Esther and Matthias had just given birth to well, not Matthias, Esther had just given birth to their second son, already had a almost two year old at home, and they were really starting to look around for things that they could give the two year old to play with on iPad.

20
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:07,840
You know, their millennial parents, very technologically savvy both in the design and art space, and didn't want to be hypocrites about using technology.

21
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,800
And so when they started to look around, they were really just finding a lot of kind of the same.

22
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:26,000
So a lot of very academic focused apps that were centered around like memorizing and regurgitating information or pretty addictive and overstimulating, more video game style things that were not appropriately designed for a two year old.

23
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:34,800
And so they started wondering, like, where are all of the beautiful, creative and safe open ended toys from the floor of our house?

24
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,360
Like, why don't those exist in a digital space yet?

25
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:57,200
And why is it that I have to be involved in helping helping my two-year-old or three-year-old or four-year-old play with any of these things because I think when it came to screen time, they were like, if we're going to put our toddler in front of a screen for 15 to 20 minutes, we want to go take a shower or make dinner or just like sit in the closet and and breathe for a minute.

26
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:17,840
And so, um, they started thinking about ways to bring that type of living room floor play, as we call it, into a digital space, which started with what ended up becoming our Busybook Toy, which was just a very open ended and creative toy with a bunch of different ide icons, no language, and you could just tap one, it would animate and make a little sound.

27
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,920
And the best part was is their son, James, could play with it completely on their own.

28
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:28,800
And they were working on it as a hobby project in the evenings and happened to show it to my brother Ryan, who is the founder of Snowman.

29
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,240
And Matt Mateus was working there at the time.

30
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:40,160
And Ryan showed it to me because I had just finished working at Disney for almost five years designing products for this exact age group.

31
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:42,080
And I was like, you guys should all chat.

32
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:43,360
This could be something interesting.

33
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:44,720
And it was.

34
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:58,960
So we started talking a lot about education and play and brand and just the fact that like all of our kids are going to grow up to have jobs that have not even been invented yet and how are we supposed to equip them for that world?

35
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:04,160
And so the education system also hasn't really changed in the last couple of hundred years.

36
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:10,640
This was pre pandemic, but even still today, there's still a lot of room to grow in education.

37
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:14,400
And so all of these things just kept coming up and coming up and coming up.

38
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:16,880
And we were talking about how we can improve.

39
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:21,200
How we really want to give kids the tools to become true creative thinkers.

40
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:24,400
So be able to really think outside of the box, think for themselves.

41
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,440
Like most toddlers don't get to make that many decisions in a day.

42
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:38,960
So we wanted to create something empowering and beautiful and that met them at their level that they could do on their own, not only for, you know, the parent to have a break for ten minutes, but also for the child.

43
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:46,720
It's a really empowering, awesome experience when a three year old can pick up something and master it on their own without help from an adult.

44
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:59,520
And so all these themes really came to together and we decided to form a company based on this mission, which was ultimately to try to help raise the next generation of kids to be creative, empowered and out of the box thinkers.

45
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:08,960
And when I came into the picture, I have to say I was so excited because I had just seen a few early sketches from Esther and I was blown away.

46
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:15,840
I had just never seen anything in this art style for kids that was so beautiful that I would hang it on my wall at home.

47
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:23,840
And it's interesting because when we first started., I had all just Disney IPs running through my head.

48
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:39,920
And after spending years playing with Elsa and Anna and Mickey and all of those great characters, it was just kind of refreshing to see something so almost minimalist and simple and gentle that I saw a huge potential in.

49
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:45,360
And so we decided, okay, we're making a company and we're going to change the world through digital play.

50
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:47,520
Yeah, that's fantastic.

51
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:50,880
I actually know your brother Ryan from like way back in the day.

52
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,640
And so we talked., gosh, like 18 months ago, very early.

53
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:56,320
I don't think you had even launched the app.

54
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:03,360
And one of the things that really struck me was that you're not just building an app.

55
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:05,920
You have a much broader vision for the company.

56
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:16,320
And so I'd love to dive into that a little bit about, you know, kind of where you see POC POC going in the future and not just being an app company.

57
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:24,560
Yeah, from the very beginning, we we realized POC POC Playroom, which is our app now was.

58
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:34,080
was something that didn't just fit into even the snowman business who make really amazing, beautiful games and apps for adults.

59
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:45,200
And actually some of them are played by children as well, but that's that's just serendipitous because we could have very easily said, okay, hey, we want to make this app under the snowman brand, but we didn't.

60
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:46,480
And that was very intentional.

61
00:06:46,800 --> 00:07:05,280
We wanted to create an entirely different company and brand centered around this creative vision because there is no like digital first creative brand that parents can wholeheartedly trust and that kids actually love.

62
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:23,200
And so we just saw this huge opportunity to bring something very premium and special and, and frankly, just different, honestly, into the world to help serve this next generation of kids who are going to be digital natives more than any of our generation ever were.

63
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:30,240
And so for us that came with this almost like need to have to create beyond the preschool age group.

64
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,880
So Pok Pok Playroom is for two to six year olds.

65
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:41,440
It does age up a little beyond six, depending on the family, but we have so many ideas that go far beyond that, up to about age twelve.

66
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:49,360
We also have ideas for adults, honestly, um, we have physical product ideas, we have like experiential ideas.

67
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:55,440
I think for the foreseeable future, we're very focused on digital, um, especially for the next like five plus years.

68
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:07,360
But, you know, don't, um, don't hold me to that because I wouldn't be surprised if one day there was a pop pop up creative beautiful space where like kids could walk in and just go wild.

69
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:29,840
We're just really inspired by all different types of playbase learning and we wanted to make sure that we can create a brand that kids can grow up with throughout their childhood and that parents can really get behind because a lot of these evergreen kids brands that have been around for decades like Lego, Disney, Pixar, and so on and so forth, they are not digital first brands.

70
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,320
They have adapted their business models to become digital.

71
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:36,240
And so we have the advantage of starting digital in a digital era.

72
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:40,160
And yeah, so for us the sky is really the limit.

73
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,800
We're really excited to continue developing not only apps but potentially other experiences as well.

74
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:45,920
Yeah.

75
00:08:46,720 --> 00:09:00,880
So I asked you that question very specifically because I think and some of our audience may have just kind of like glossed over and like, oh, this she's just talking about some like, you know, branding stuff and oh, she worked at Disney or whatever.

76
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:21,520
But I think this is a really important thing that more subscription apps should be thinking about is that part of what makes a company like Disney so successful, and you're right, they kind of came at it not from digital, but came into digital, but Disney Plus is so successful in part because of the combination of so many different business lines.

77
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:26,640
You know, my son and I are watching through Star Wars on Disney Plus, and guess what?

78
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:29,440
We both would love to go to their Star Wars experience.

79
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:31,680
It's crazy expensive, so we're not going to do it.

80
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,040
You know, maybe one day.

81
00:09:36,160 --> 00:10:14,320
Um, but so there's experiences, you know, we bought him a, uh, Mandalorian phone case, uh, you know, and so you have digital, you have physical products, you have physical experiences, you have digital experiences, you have digital products, you have games, you have IP, you have licensing, and it's such a broad business and I think too many subscription app companies, consumer subscription companies just have the blinders on of like all our business is ever going to be is how many new users can we get into the app and how many of those can we get turn into subscribers and how many can we turn into retained subscribers over as many years as possible?

82
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:19,360
And like you said, right now, POCPOC's focus is the Playroom app.

83
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,800
And so you're very focused on those metrics and those things.

84
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:46,640
But I think in the long run, and so anybody listening to this who glossed over what Melissa was talking about, go rewind the podcast, listen again, think about your own business and think about, you know, are there ways that we can think beyond what we're currently building and have a larger vision to do something in the world to, to, to, and to, I mean, frankly, to expand LTV, right?

85
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:48,960
Like absolutely, absolutely.

86
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:55,920
It's funny too because we are, and we'll, so we can talk about this, you know, when we talk more about product optimization at some point, I'm sure we'll get to that.

87
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:09,280
And, but we are, we are willing to make sacrifices on optimization for brand longevity and experience because we feel that, yes, on the one hand, we are a very new company.

88
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:18,000
We have a new product long way to go with it, and we're very invested in that, but we are also invested in the future.

89
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:24,560
We have an entire sort of hive mind at the company that are just focused on long term things.

90
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:26,240
That's why we do a lot of PR.

91
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:32,320
That's why we do a lot of organic marketing as well, because we're not in this just to make a quick buck and move on.

92
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:34,960
We really do want to redefine the space.

93
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:44,720
And I think the best way to do that as a kids brand is to be as multidimensional as possible, because we also feel that if we do that, we're going to be as good as we can.

94
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:54,080
earn the trust of parents, and I say that like very seriously because earning trust from parents is very special and important and difficult.

95
00:11:54,640 --> 00:12:04,560
But if we can do that and do right for them and their kids, then I think we really have an endless opportunity to grow with that family, which would be a privilege.

96
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:07,520
Like I know, growing up, we were a very Disney household.

97
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:09,680
No big surprise that I ended up working there.

98
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:12,080
That is, it's formational.

99
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:13,120
It defines you.

100
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:15,840
And we don't have digital brands that are doing that right now.

101
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:20,400
We have digital products that are doing that, like Minecraft, Roblox, other things.

102
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:29,040
But, you know, there's a huge opportunity to scope beyond that and build experiences that the whole family can really enjoy.

103
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:37,120
And so for us, we're very focused on the playground, but we're also just as mindful of what we're doing for the long term.

104
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:38,640
That's fantastic.

105
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:40,320
And that's part of the thing, right?

106
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:51,840
Having that broader vision helps shape your decisions day in and day out that you're not just, you know, overly optimizing or using scammy tactics or things like that, you're building something for the long haul.

107
00:12:52,560 --> 00:13:14,560
Speaking of building things, I did want to kind of get into so you you know, you met the co founders, you got super excited about this single kind of app experience, you know, built a much grander vision, but how did you go from that prototype to then building a now award winning, um, successful experience?

108
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:17,760
So it came, I mean the people.

109
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:23,040
I mean, that's the fundamental ingredient to any successful product or company.

110
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:28,240
We hired a really diverse group of people that are not all from the games or kids space.

111
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:32,000
We have and we worked with a lot of kids.

112
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:43,440
So the POC POC playroom is essentially a digital playroom filled with open-ended toys that spark imagination and creative thinking and learning through play that the child leads themselves.

113
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:58,800
So because we have no menus, no language, no instructions, no winning or losing, no points to score, all those things made it actually extremely tough to to design because we had to think like toddlers and that's impossibly difficult as an adult.

114
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:00,000
We're just so jaded.

115
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:06,960
So we had to really tap into the sense of wonder that we all had growing up and work with a lot of family.

116
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:10,160
So we played with a lot of physical toys.

117
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:18,320
We go to a lot of toy stores, toy fairs, museums, science centers, and we talk to each other, share stories from our childhood.

118
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:24,960
We go to daycare and toyschool and play with kids and really just try to understand what makes play a reality.

119
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:29,200
play so much fun for them and so educational and valuable for them.

120
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:33,840
And then we take that and we wrap it up and we start playtesting and building prototypes.

121
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:49,680
And eventually the play room became pretty full, but we do have like a pretty robust development process where we work together internally first and then we go outside, we test with a bunch of families, we go to schools and test, we come back, we learn.

122
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:52,640
I mean, kids are brutally honest.

123
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,840
Under the age of six I would say they have zero filter.

124
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:56,560
It's wonderful.

125
00:14:57,120 --> 00:15:00,080
I think we all learn a lot from them every day when they're like, no.

126
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:05,200
So I think that has been like the secret ingredient to our success.

127
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:14,960
And then, of course, we try to integrate as much data as possible into all of that because at the end of the day, we want to make sure that we're fully optimized for success.

128
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:19,920
And sometimes that means following our gut and sometimes that means following the data.

129
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:28,240
But we find if we follow the kids, they tend to lead us to the best path forward because they just, they just know.

130
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:38,080
Yeah, it's so one fascinating thing to me and my kids and I played with the app quite a bit during the debate, whatever, you know, long, long time ago.

131
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:48,240
But in thinking through how to make data informed decisions for a product like Pok Pok Playroom, how do you instrument that?

132
00:15:49,440 --> 00:16:00,880
Are you looking at time spent and touches and things like that to kind of understand, you know, is this and for those who haven't played with the app, you should especially.

133
00:16:01,120 --> 00:16:02,240
if you have kids, it's really cool.

134
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:12,720
But it's essentially you land on this kind of home screen with a bunch of different little icons essentially that take you into different toys.

135
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:16,320
And each toy is just this kind of open exploration.

136
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:19,680
One is a drawing toy and there's a bunch of other little toys.

137
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:25,520
But kids naturally do start this just tapping around and seeing things that move.

138
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:30,320
And Pok Pok makes noise and things move and you can play little games.

139
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:35,760
I mean, it's like a Lego combined with a Lego combined with blocks combined with, like, there's so many fun experiences.

140
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:41,120
Sorry, pitching the product, but I think it's somewhat helpful for people to understand that aspect of it.

141
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:56,720
So do you track like touches and like minutes spent in the different experiences and how do you collect that data and then how do you kind of make decisions based on that and reconcile that against kind of what you're seeing in playtesting?

142
00:16:57,680 --> 00:16:58,640
If I can jump on that.

143
00:16:59,120 --> 00:16:59,200
Yeah.

144
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:04,720
Basically, like we're like our tech stack is growing, but we've been leveraging.

145
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:10,640
Amplitude for like a lot of these data analytics, uh, purposes.

146
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:14,240
But the answer to what you say is yes to basically a lot of things.

147
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:21,360
We do track when, uh, kids are playing with toys, uh, how long they're playing or like the frequency of toys.

148
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:24,800
Of course, all of that is like extremely like anonymized.

149
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:34,720
We're very, very, very careful when it comes to collecting data because just, uh, we want to be very protective of the people who use our app.

150
00:17:35,440 --> 00:17:36,640
We're COPA compliant.

151
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:47,840
We also like dealing with KidSafe, which is an organization that makes sure that our app is very KidSafe and kind of keeps us on our toes in that way.

152
00:17:48,320 --> 00:18:10,160
So yeah, basically we look at a lot of those datas, but it's interesting because, like Melissa said, we're not necessarily an app that is very focused on gaming and getting kids addicted to it because the purpose of it is not necessarily to boost the length of the session like more and more or to boost the number of sessions.

153
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:14,560
Like we don't want kids to necessarily use Puck Puck six times a day for an hour.

154
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:15,920
That's not the purpose.

155
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:23,040
We want for when the kids use the app that they're entertained by it, of course, because otherwise it wouldn't serve its purpose.

156
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:26,000
But at the same time, we don't want them to be addicted.

157
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:30,880
We want it when a parent needs to make supper, they need to give the tablet for thirty minutes.

158
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:32,640
The kids have an awesome experience.

159
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:34,080
They learn, they play.

160
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:40,960
But at the same time, another thing we're really focused on is that when a parent has to take the iPad back because they're not able to use it.

161
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:41,760
because supper's ready.

162
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:54,960
We don't want to have a tantrum, which is also playing in that if the kids is super overstimulated by like getting coins and like just like focused on an objective, that might very well happen.

163
00:18:55,360 --> 00:19:14,800
So that's very much part of how we're looking at data, like we want to make sure that we balance qualitative and quantitative data to create this experience that is calming, entertaining, educative, but also not fall into just put KPIs that are pushing us to like get kids to play longer and more often.

164
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:16,640
That's something we're very careful about.

165
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:18,160
It's true.

166
00:19:18,480 --> 00:19:29,360
I just want to add as well that we definitely want kids to want to play with Pok Pok, but we don't want it to be that their Kryptonite, you know, because that that's fun for no one.

167
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:36,320
Like, of course, we would much rather and with our own kids even like go outside, play in the mud, get dirty, have, make a mess.

168
00:19:36,720 --> 00:19:40,800
Like that is more important than any digital toy we're ever going to make, probably.

169
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:43,920
But there's a time and a place for digital play.

170
00:19:44,320 --> 00:20:10,080
And when we bring it into the hands of children, when we're looking at the data, we just want to make sure that they're getting the most robust experience possible, that they're moving from toy to toy, that they're not like drowning in front of it for hours and hours and hours, and that they want to come back because one of the, and one of the ways we actually figure that out, it was it was kind of interesting because balancing engagement with addiction is very counterintuitive.

171
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:15,360
Like most apps are just focused on engagement and like how can they bring you back, bring you back, bring you back.

172
00:20:15,520 --> 00:20:16,720
And of course, that is important to us.

173
00:20:17,920 --> 00:20:24,720
in order to like keep a healthy business, but we want to make sure we're doing it in a way that's healthy for the children and also feels good for the parents.

174
00:20:25,040 --> 00:20:30,560
And so with that, we decided that all of our toys will be intrinsically motivated.

175
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:41,120
So the play comes from the child, the any limit or boundary that they encounter while they're playing is something that is within themselves, not within the app.

176
00:20:41,520 --> 00:20:50,400
And there's no congratulatory anything that happens when they do anything, like even solving a problem like taking a mail and putting it in a mailbox.

177
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:54,720
That's only a problem to solve if a child identifies it as a problem to solve.

178
00:20:54,960 --> 00:21:03,280
And that's why it's such a beautiful thing because we can see that kids are able to deliver the mail, let's say, for ten minutes.

179
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:06,960
And then let's say mom calls and says, Okay, kids, it's time to get out of the car.

180
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:08,400
We're at our destination.

181
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:10,240
They can just put it away.

182
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:16,080
And when they come back, there'll still be more mail to deliver if they so choose, or they can go do something completely different.

183
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:22,160
So because of that open endedness, it actually works in our favor in terms of engagement because they're curious.

184
00:21:22,240 --> 00:21:29,440
They want to come back and see what else they can do, but they're not fighting because there's not like one more thing to do before the time runs out.

185
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:36,960
And so it's a very tricky dichotomy and we're looking at the data a lot in order to make sure that we're constantly finding the right balance there.

186
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:37,760
Yeah.

187
00:21:38,480 --> 00:21:41,200
I want to highlight again because I think this is just so interesting.

188
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:46,880
I hope that, you know, our audience is probably not a ton of kids apps.

189
00:21:47,040 --> 00:21:50,800
And so for the kids apps, they're probably just eating this up and loving this conversation.

190
00:21:51,680 --> 00:22:01,920
If you are listening to this and hopefully you didn't skip this one and you're not a kids app, again, go back and listen to that whole sequence in the frame of building for adults as well.

191
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:13,280
It's part of the beauty of a subscription app where your customers are paying you versus ads being paid for the maximum amount of time that you can spend in an app.

192
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:23,920
For me, this has been a real shift in the app economy is that the subscription model is more aligned with delivering value than capturing attention.

193
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:37,360
So when you think about your KPIs not as a kids app but as a scanner app or whatever, it's like going back and listen to that description again through the eyes of how does that play into adults?

194
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:39,680
Like do we need this like gamification?

195
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:48,720
Do we need rewarding engagement versus like just delivering the value and getting them in and out of the app quickly and things along those lines?

196
00:22:49,280 --> 00:23:08,240
And again, I think it's so fun having this conversation because I think there's just so much to take away, not just for kids apps, which again, I think the kidsids apps listening are going to love this, but I think so many other subscription app entrepreneurs can learn from the approach that you're taking and the playtesting and balancing against being data informed and all those things.

197
00:23:08,480 --> 00:23:11,360
So, so just fantastic, fantastic insights there.

198
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:17,680
To not drag this on for hours, I would love to talk to you all for like multiple hours, but we only have another thirty minutes or so.

199
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:22,480
I did want to step into pricing and the subscription model.

200
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:25,040
You know, again, this is a subscription app podcast.

201
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:37,200
All of us have kind of made this decision already, but again, I think it's going to be informative to step through your decision to make it a subscription first, and then we can talk about pricing and paywall and things like that.

202
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:47,840
But having been on the beta and played with it with my kids, there is maybe an obvious thing to do in that each different experience is kind of a toy.

203
00:23:48,560 --> 00:23:58,160
And just like you buy a toy, you might just buy each of those individual experiences for five bucks or ten bucks or two dollars ninety nine or whatever it is.

204
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:05,360
So I'm curious, as you've built a product., played tested, talked to parents, I'm sure you did some kind of price discovery.

205
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:19,200
But what was the thinking and research and decision making behind deciding to go for subscription versus single and app purchase or even lifetime or any other business model that you could have potentially chosen?

206
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:24,480
So this was a huge conversation that lasted a very long time.

207
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:31,200
And honestly, sometimes we're still going back to it again and again to just question it and make sure that we're really on the right path.

208
00:24:31,440 --> 00:24:32,560
But fundamentally.

209
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:40,960
Fundamentally speaking, we knew that we wanted to create something that was always evolving and that because kids are so curious that they never stop being curious.

210
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:45,120
So we knew we had to create new content regularly and we also had a million ideas.

211
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:46,880
So we were like, we have to keep making stuff.

212
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:50,640
And to do that, we need recurring revenue.

213
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:53,840
So a big piece of the why came from that.

214
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:56,480
It was just like, we know we want to make more content over time.

215
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:58,480
So we should be a subscription.

216
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:00,080
That was like step one.

217
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:04,240
There were probably fifty other steps in there of like, you know, really digging into that.

218
00:25:04,800 --> 00:25:14,480
But at the end of the day, we want to make sure that we're able to create value because, and that value is interesting because we, our user is not our customer.

219
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:16,960
So we need value for the parent and value for the child.

220
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:31,760
And if we were to say paywall certain experiences or charge per toy, it becomes very confusing for a toddler who is holding this device in his hand and saying, why can't I play?

221
00:25:32,880 --> 00:25:33,920
What did I do wrong?

222
00:25:34,480 --> 00:25:36,160
And that is not something that they have done wrong.

223
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:44,560
That is our, that would be poor design in my opinion because the child should never face a boundary when they're playing like that.

224
00:25:45,440 --> 00:25:49,680
Maybe the boundary is, oh, I can't pick up this giant rock outside because it's just too heavy.

225
00:25:49,920 --> 00:25:54,960
That's very different than, like, I can't touch the rock because someone's asking me to pay for it first.

226
00:25:55,840 --> 00:26:01,360
So we wanted to create the absolute purest experience for kids from day one.

227
00:26:02,160 --> 00:26:08,640
That has been challenging, to be honest, because there are tricks of the trade, which I'm sure a lot of the people listening.

228
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:11,040
know, in order to make more money.

229
00:26:11,600 --> 00:26:20,560
But for the very beginning of our journey, we wanted to make sure we're starting with a very, very clean slate that's creating the most value for the child first.

230
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:24,640
And then for the parent, it goes back to the added content.

231
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:33,200
I think that's a huge piece and also understanding that our team doesn't just rescan existing toys with seasonal stuff.

232
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:34,400
Sure.

233
00:26:34,720 --> 00:26:44,800
Sometimes, like recently, we'll do a Halloween update because kids actually think that's so not the fundamental core of our product development.

234
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:49,280
That is inventing new toys that have never existed in the world before now.

235
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:52,240
And that takes a lot of time and effort and money.

236
00:26:52,720 --> 00:27:03,680
So the subscription, the hard paywall, which we can talk about, the subscription model itself, those are all things that we found would be best balanced for the child and for the parent.

237
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:05,920
And I know Felix probably has some thoughts on this as well.

238
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:06,960
Yeah.

239
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:12,880
I mean, it is all in a way, like we're asking parents to commit right away to a subscription.

240
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:22,240
Like we do offer a free trial because that's very important that the kid can try the app and actually see if it's a good fit, if they like it, if they learn with it.

241
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:39,200
But at the same time, like Melissa said, we do want to we're kind of betting on parents who value having a very pure experience where there's no paywall, there's no sneaky like game mechanics that the kids might fall into and then they get that crazy bill because of in-app purchases.

242
00:27:39,840 --> 00:27:43,120
We really wanted to go against that one hundred percent.

243
00:27:43,280 --> 00:27:51,200
And also I think it's beneficial for the parents because again, we want to create something that the child can play with without the parents being involved.

244
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:56,720
And there's kind of a side effect with paywalls in a freemium version is that the kid's going to run into paywall.

245
00:27:57,120 --> 00:28:00,800
Two to six, he might not even be able to read like at that level, right?

246
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:02,640
So like he doesn't know what's happening.

247
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:06,320
Then he really needs one of the parents to help him get out of there, you know?

248
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:16,960
So like that was another big reflection and talking to user a lot like the kids get so much fun out of having a new toy in a playroom.

249
00:28:17,360 --> 00:28:48,960
and like a new big update like we did for Halloween where like everything was like wrapped in Halloween and there's like monsters and there was like like like pumpkins and it was really great and kids like really loved that so we do want to create like that delight often and subscription works in that way you know because otherwise it would be hard to to make that constant delight and new toys and innovation happen in a business model that is just strictly paid so that kind of like went on to this interview.

250
00:28:49,440 --> 00:28:52,560
But like Melissa said, it's taking that decision.

251
00:28:53,040 --> 00:28:54,880
We take kind of a hard stance on that.

252
00:28:55,920 --> 00:28:57,360
But that's what we're betting on.

253
00:28:57,600 --> 00:28:59,360
We want to create the best experience as possible.

254
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:00,640
Yeah.

255
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:15,200
And it's just, it's so interesting how the subscription model helps align with that experience that you're, you're, and it, and it, as you were saying, Melissa, that, you know, your goal is to deliver value.

256
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:19,600
And if somebody's not getting value, they cancel it.

257
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:24,480
It's something I think, you know, we're seeing a lot in our revenue cat data.

258
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:26,800
You know, consumers are very rational.

259
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:40,960
You know, there's a metric I like to call zombie subscribers where, and we've looked at this a few times in our data, and there's just not that many people who stay subscribed to something that they're not actively using.

260
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:47,840
And then there's there's some apps where you won't necessarily, you know, open the app, you know, widget apps and other things like that.

261
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:48,800
So, yeah.

262
00:29:49,280 --> 00:30:00,480
Or you pay for it on Stripe, um, and you open it on your Apple or you pay for it on Apple and you're using it somewhere else or, you know, things like that where, you know, it's not necessarily direct reflection.

263
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:05,440
But generally, you know, if you're delivering value, consumers stay subscribed.

264
00:30:05,600 --> 00:30:07,760
If they're not getting value, they cancel.

265
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:13,360
And it's just a very like direct alignment of incentives.

266
00:30:13,840 --> 00:30:20,240
And one of the things I did want to dive a little deeper on, and you kind of mentioned it, you both mentioned it in passing, is that you now have a hard paywall.

267
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:22,880
So I was on the beta, you know, me and my kids played with it.

268
00:30:23,280 --> 00:30:32,240
But it wasn't until this this week that I realized that you went downloading the app from the App Store to like better understand and get off the beta, you know, the full experience.

269
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:40,320
And so I downloaded it and you can't do anything without starting a free trial, which I mean, more and more apps are going this direction.

270
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:42,800
I'm actually going to be experimenting with a hard paywall myself.

271
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:54,000
So I wanted to dive into this a little bit around the decision making and then even whatever you're willing to share on kind of how it's performing and if you've experimented with any other variations.

272
00:30:56,320 --> 00:30:58,560
Felix, I know this is something you're very passionate about.

273
00:31:00,640 --> 00:31:06,160
Yeah, I mean, having worked for other apps, like having a hard paywall is a big decision, right?

274
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:11,200
Because like you're going to lose a lot of people who don't want to enter a free trial and that's totally understandable.

275
00:31:12,320 --> 00:31:17,120
So the thinking behind that was a lot of like creating that really pure experience.

276
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,280
But we tried a few things with that paywall.

277
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:29,520
The first one we launched was extremely simple, kind of like explaining value proposition that we have in the app.

278
00:31:29,920 --> 00:31:34,560
And then at that point, if people were ready to commit, they could enter a trial.

279
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:37,360
And if they don't like it, they can just cancel, right?

280
00:31:37,680 --> 00:31:39,360
Like it's fairly easy to do.

281
00:31:40,400 --> 00:31:55,920
So based on that, like we wanted to push that the performance was quite good, to be honest, like with a hard paywall, what's good is like your download to paid rate is generally a bit higher than if you let people the opportunity to test the toys.

282
00:31:56,320 --> 00:31:56,480
Right.

283
00:31:57,120 --> 00:32:06,880
But one thing that we impressed me is that we saw we we did lose a lot of people who were like, listen, I don't know if my kids are going to like it.

284
00:32:07,120 --> 00:32:11,680
I'm not ready to put in my credit card information and enter a trial.

285
00:32:12,400 --> 00:32:19,120
So we wanted to see if we let them try the toys before entering the trial, would that make any difference?

286
00:32:19,280 --> 00:32:23,520
Would they see the toys and be like, wow, like I really, really need to come in for a trial.

287
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:31,040
So we designed a paywall where we let users try the toys for twenty seconds at a time and then the toy would close.

288
00:32:32,160 --> 00:32:37,200
that would let them try the toys and hopefully like it and want to see more about it.

289
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:41,280
But interestingly enough, that kind of backfire in a way.

290
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:52,240
We got a lot of people trying that aspect, but a lot of them just kind of stuck with that, they saw that experience in itself as a free trial.

291
00:32:52,640 --> 00:33:00,880
And they kind of like we saw we had a lot of users like just playing the twenty seconds all over again.

292
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:03,840
And when we looked at the data, we're like, that's crazy.

293
00:33:04,240 --> 00:33:08,960
Like there was a huge amount of people who did that and were quite satisfied with that.

294
00:33:09,440 --> 00:33:14,480
The reasoning is it's not the parents who would suffer going through the twenty seconds again and again.

295
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:17,040
It was the kid because they would be playing with that.

296
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:32,000
So interestingly enough that creates a lot more active users in a way, but it did perform more poorly because like the kids would just play with that and no one was really winning because kids were playing in a really lesser version of the app.

297
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:36,160
We were not getting more trials and parents, I guess, like were in.

298
00:33:36,240 --> 00:33:40,080
and be able to give their kids really what the app could focus on.

299
00:33:40,320 --> 00:33:52,960
So based on that, we we rolled it back, uh, of course, and now we're trying a lot of different paywalls like AB testing, uh, really trying different value proposition, which is really hard because it's a hard paywall.

300
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:57,200
Parents have to get it and some parents get it when we talk to people.

301
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:58,960
Some people get it from the app store.

302
00:33:59,120 --> 00:33:59,520
They read it.

303
00:33:59,600 --> 00:34:01,360
They're like, I know what they're doing.

304
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:06,960
They want to create this calming, safe, uh, app that is open and that is beautiful, that is calming.

305
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:09,840
But like some people don't necessararily get it right away.

306
00:34:10,080 --> 00:34:20,560
So a lot of the things we're doing is really spelling out the value proposition in a way that people will understand and that will make them want to try the free trial.

307
00:34:20,800 --> 00:34:22,800
And at that point, we're really talking to parents.

308
00:34:23,040 --> 00:34:27,200
So we have a lot of AB tests coming in that direction.

309
00:34:27,840 --> 00:34:38,080
And I think just to add quickly, like that is the kind of crux of our challenge being that having a user that isn't our customer because we thought, parents, we'll give you a tour of the playlist.

310
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:40,720
Take a look around, follow your nose, see what's up.

311
00:34:41,840 --> 00:34:44,160
And a parent saw that and were like, Oh, here it is.

312
00:34:44,320 --> 00:34:45,040
Here you go, Johnny.

313
00:34:45,280 --> 00:34:54,320
And then they just walk away and their kids would be stuck with this thing and they would get frustrated because every twenty seconds they would be popped back to the play room from one toy.

314
00:34:54,560 --> 00:35:01,760
They could always go back in, but this thing that we designed as like a beautiful tour for the parents was totally misinterpreted and that was our fault.

315
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:04,560
Like we just didn't design it well enough for them to get it.

316
00:35:05,120 --> 00:35:07,200
And it was a really big learning for us.

317
00:35:07,360 --> 00:35:15,360
It's something we might actually go back and test again with different design and better communication that explains just more clearly like what this is.

318
00:35:15,680 --> 00:35:24,000
But we're for now, like Felix said, focused more on optimizing the hard paywall and making sure that we're communicating the value correctly to parents.

319
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,240
And that's kind of different for every parent.

320
00:35:26,560 --> 00:35:38,480
Some parents just want an app that's going to keep their kid busy for half an hour, and others want something that they can tangibly come back with and say, Oh, my kid learned X, Y, and Z in the last 27 minutes.

321
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:38,880
Cool.

322
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:40,400
Now I feel like a awesome parent.

323
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:43,360
Or, you know, there's just a multitude of things.

324
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:47,040
So we're just in the midst of massive testing on that front.

325
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:48,560
Very cool.

326
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:52,400
I'm curious if you've experimented with video paywalls.

327
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:55,120
Is that something you've done a test on yet or is that forthcoming?

328
00:35:55,440 --> 00:35:56,240
It's forthcoming.

329
00:35:56,400 --> 00:35:57,840
It's in the works right now, actually.

330
00:35:58,960 --> 00:35:59,280
Very cool.

331
00:35:59,520 --> 00:35:59,600
Yeah.

332
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:00,080
Yeah.

333
00:36:00,480 --> 00:36:02,800
The hard paywall thing is just so fascinating.

334
00:36:03,520 --> 00:36:09,360
I think, you know, we really are still kind of in the early stages of figuring this out for consumer products.

335
00:36:09,520 --> 00:36:16,720
I mean, you know, on the web and SaaS and kind of other business models that kind of have a lot more maturity in experimenting with all these things.

336
00:36:18,240 --> 00:36:29,680
And I've just been seeing more and more apps experiment with not even a hard paywall with a free trial, but even like a long onboarding and like subscribe or not and no freemium experience.

337
00:36:29,920 --> 00:36:37,840
And then on the flip side, I mean, there's apps where a freemium experience actually really delivers additional value and helps with word of mouth.

338
00:36:37,920 --> 00:36:41,520
And like there's just so many different ways to run these businesses.

339
00:36:41,760 --> 00:36:45,840
So it's cool to see as a kids app experimenting with this hard paywall.

340
00:36:46,080 --> 00:36:54,960
And I imagine, again, having played with with the app myself a bit, I imagine the free trial ends up being a kind of free trial for both the parents and the kids.

341
00:36:55,600 --> 00:37:05,520
The parents like, because they have to start the free trial to even unlock any experience in the app, they're there with the device.

342
00:37:06,240 --> 00:37:10,560
And when I first introduced it to my kids, I sat down and played too.

343
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:11,600
They're actually really fun.

344
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:16,960
You move the little things around and you accomplish things and you get little audio rewards and stuff.

345
00:37:17,440 --> 00:37:20,320
It's cool that you're thinking about adult toys too, because I think, you know.

346
00:37:20,400 --> 00:37:23,120
You know, too often we adults don't play enough.

347
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:30,320
And so sitting on the floor playing the with the play room with my kids was actually a really fun experience.

348
00:37:30,480 --> 00:37:37,920
So do you think about that, the kind of combination of like how much of this is a kind of a parent free trial and how much of it is a kid's free trial?

349
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:40,960
Do you have any way to like determine that or user testing or anything?

350
00:37:41,600 --> 00:37:44,400
It's so hard to actually know who's holding the device, right?

351
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:52,720
But we it's something we do try to experiment with in user testing and we just ask as many people as we'll listen to us.

352
00:37:53,280 --> 00:37:53,920
and answer us.

353
00:37:54,560 --> 00:38:04,560
But it's funny because we, one of the other challenges that we have is that sometimes parents don't understand what to do because they've forgotten how to play.

354
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:08,160
Um, and as adults, that's very normal.

355
00:38:08,480 --> 00:38:15,120
Like if it's very normal to forget how to play as an adult, but, um, you know, so they'll ask us like, what's the point?

356
00:38:15,280 --> 00:38:16,800
Or like, how do they do it?

357
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:18,400
How do I teach my kid how to use this?

358
00:38:18,560 --> 00:38:20,320
And we're like, you don't have to teach them anything.

359
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:25,840
Just hand them the device and let them follow their nose and you'll see the creationativity come to life.

360
00:38:26,080 --> 00:38:26,960
You'll see them have fun.

361
00:38:27,200 --> 00:38:28,080
You'll see them explore.

362
00:38:28,320 --> 00:38:29,680
And that's all we want.

363
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:38,480
But we're so ingrained as a society to be like winning and achieving something and trying to get somewhere with everything that we do.

364
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:43,760
Even if it's like listening to a podcast, I listen to Subclub all the time and it's for my own development.

365
00:38:44,080 --> 00:38:45,200
It's to get smarter.

366
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:46,320
It's to learn something.

367
00:38:47,040 --> 00:38:50,000
And it's so rare that I listen to a podcast just to relax.

368
00:38:50,320 --> 00:39:01,120
Like, we're just so focused on achieving things that it's really tough for us sometimes to communicate that to parents and the best way to do it is to show them is to have them play with it themselves.

369
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:04,480
But funny enough, the barrier can be, can be hard.

370
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:10,000
Like when we see it in person during testing, they're like, Oh, well, how do I play with how do I play with this one?

371
00:39:10,240 --> 00:39:29,680
And you can see them being embarrassed, like, I don't know how to play and it's almost, it's almost sad, honestly, but like, it's actually a serious problem that we're, we're trying to combat all the time because if you give your kid like an ABC thing, like, hey, hey, Johnny, like practice the ABCs for ten minutes, it's very easy for a parent to walk their child through that.

372
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:37,600
But if you get a set of just blank Lego blocks, like the simple set without instructions, what do you do?

373
00:39:37,760 --> 00:39:38,560
What do you tell your kids?

374
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:45,120
Like, go, like, make something or throw them against the wall and see if you can land them in the garbage bin.

375
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:46,960
Like, there's so many ways to play with that.

376
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:48,560
And that's what Pok Pok is.

377
00:39:48,720 --> 00:39:54,640
And that's our biggest challenge in the onboarding and getting parents to understand and getting them playing with it as well.

378
00:39:55,680 --> 00:40:00,640
And then the last part of this hard paywall experience I wanted to talk about is the price.

379
00:40:01,840 --> 00:40:09,440
So the app or at least what I saw, I don't know if you're doing AB testing currently, but I saw a seven dollar a month and 46 dollars a year.

380
00:40:10,160 --> 00:40:18,160
I'm curious kind of the thought and research behind the pricing and then if you have done or plan on doing price experimentation.

381
00:40:19,360 --> 00:40:27,680
Yeah, well, when we first launched in May 2021, like this is also like a pretty recent app, we're just went over to one year mark.

382
00:40:28,160 --> 00:40:32,160
So we started at a price that was like 29,99 a year.

383
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:38,320
year, uh, 699, uh, a month, uh, 399, uh, pardon me, 399 a month, that's a new price.

384
00:40:38,400 --> 00:40:40,640
Uh, and there was a lot of thinking that went into that.

385
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:49,440
So looking at other apps with like similar value, the amount of toys that we had in the app at that point, uh, what people would be willing to do.

386
00:40:50,000 --> 00:40:57,120
And honestly, like the first price was pretty dead on in, in, in, in, meaning that like we got a really, really great adoption from the get-go.

387
00:40:57,360 --> 00:40:58,960
And like the stats were really good.

388
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:01,120
The download to paid was really good.

389
00:41:01,600 --> 00:41:04,720
Uh, but as we kind of expanded the player.

390
00:41:04,720 --> 00:41:10,080
The playroom like doubling its size basically like we're like, oh, we think we brought a lot of value.

391
00:41:10,240 --> 00:41:12,320
There's new updates every other week.

392
00:41:12,640 --> 00:41:14,960
So we wanted to test another price.

393
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:21,120
So we went pretty drastically and we doubled the price to see just like where does that what does that do?

394
00:41:21,440 --> 00:41:30,960
And interestingly enough, it actually made her download to pay better, uh, which was quite surprising and somehow like counterintuitive.

395
00:41:31,520 --> 00:41:33,360
So that was a good learning.

396
00:41:33,600 --> 00:41:36,720
Like I think people when they see something really qualitative, they're ready to.

397
00:41:37,280 --> 00:41:37,440
pay.

398
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:44,480
Like, and unless the price is like crazy, crazy different, uh, we found that it worked pretty well for us.

399
00:41:44,960 --> 00:42:03,280
But, of course, like everything in life, there was like, uh, there was a backlash in terms of like retention, especially when we talk about monthly because people, especially, you know, when they're on the monthly iOS subscription every week, every month, they'll get that email and like, kind of rethink like, okay, that's how much I'm getting charged.

400
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:06,960
So now we're kind of trying to find a sweet spot.

401
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:08,720
Uh, we like you said the price is.

402
00:42:09,040 --> 00:42:10,400
is kind of in between right now.

403
00:42:11,360 --> 00:42:20,800
We want to do more price testing and we want to find a price also that will enable us to do paid acquisition at a good level, you know?

404
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:31,520
So like we're working a lot on optimizing our metrics, but we also want to have a good LTV that will allow us to, um, to unblock certain paid channels.

405
00:42:31,760 --> 00:42:34,080
So like that's kind of where our thinking is.

406
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:40,560
And also we want to be conscious of how much people are able to pay also with like we're.

407
00:42:41,840 --> 00:42:48,960
in a special economic situation where we want to be mindful of that as well, so that goes into our decision for pricing.

408
00:42:49,760 --> 00:42:54,240
Yeah, it's fascinating that you brought up retention as a function of pricing.

409
00:42:55,440 --> 00:43:06,160
So, if I'm understanding you correctly, just to make sure when you doubled the price, you more than doubled your revenue because your download to paid went up and you doubled the price.

410
00:43:06,320 --> 00:43:10,240
So you actually more than doubled your revenue by making that one pricing change.

411
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:12,400
Yeah, it was a pretty big win.

412
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:16,480
But we were very, very excited about it.

413
00:43:16,640 --> 00:43:23,360
But, um, like Felix said, funny enough, we also did that during, um, we did that in February of 2022.

414
00:43:23,760 --> 00:43:28,800
So that's when the world started to come to pieces in many ways as well.

415
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:34,000
So, and then the other thing is kids tend to use their devices a bit less in the summer.

416
00:43:34,160 --> 00:43:37,120
They're busy playing outside and swimming and stuff and having a lot of fun.

417
00:43:37,360 --> 00:43:43,040
So with that, after several months, we started to see an uptick in our monthly churn.

418
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:48,320
after talking to users and really trying to dig into what factors might have been causing it.

419
00:43:48,480 --> 00:44:06,080
Like, we're very confident that it was the price and it could be, you know, we split test everything, but, um, at the end of the day, we, we can't say one hundred percent if it's just that it's high or if it's just that it's high during this exact time in, like, the macroeconomic situation.

420
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:19,040
So we're, we're constantly iterating, but we're, we're in this sort of sweet spot pricing right now that we're feeling good about that it seems users are also happy with that, uh, in terms of value for them, which is obviously very, very important.

421
00:44:19,440 --> 00:44:23,360
So yeah, it's just a constant testing game.

422
00:44:24,320 --> 00:44:35,120
Yeah, this is a topic I hope to delve a little more into, especially with like you all were saying, you know, where we're at kind of in the broader macroeconomic environment.

423
00:44:37,280 --> 00:44:45,280
But one of the things it is really tough to do is to go back and look at how these kind of experiments, you know, impact retention.

424
00:44:45,440 --> 00:44:48,400
And so it's really cool that you all did actually take the time to do that.

425
00:44:49,120 --> 00:44:57,840
Because really with a subscription app, it's not just about that first conversion, it's about building a long term relationship with these customers.

426
00:44:58,880 --> 00:45:03,040
And price is a huge part of that relationship that you're building with the customers.

427
00:45:03,200 --> 00:45:18,800
And there is a balance between that, the premium aspect of building a premium product and charging a premium for it, but then also being affordable for the long haul where you're delivering more value to the user than you're asking in return.

428
00:45:19,680 --> 00:45:24,880
And then those economics start to shift when broader economics start to shift.

429
00:45:25,760 --> 00:45:34,320
I think it's going to be really interesting over the next, you know, 12 to 18 months of experimenting with potentially lowering prices, you know, finding those sweet spots.

430
00:45:34,480 --> 00:45:37,440
So it's great to hear that you're already experimenting with that.

431
00:45:37,920 --> 00:45:41,200
Gosh, there's so much I want to ask, but we just have a few minutes left.

432
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:51,440
One of the things Pok Pok has done especially well that I think a lot of apps struggle with is getting pressed, getting featured by Apple.

433
00:45:52,800 --> 00:45:56,240
Melissa, you've been on a ton of podcasts, you've gotten a ton of articles written.

434
00:45:57,520 --> 00:46:18,160
Give me a speedrun of advice to other subscription app entrepreneurs of how to tell good stories and then, and then, you know, which things you found to be more impactful for the business and networking versus things that maybe you put a lot of effort into that didn't actually move the needle.

435
00:46:18,560 --> 00:46:18,720
Sure.

436
00:46:18,800 --> 00:46:18,880
Yeah.

437
00:46:19,120 --> 00:46:22,720
I could talk about this for hours, but I'll give you a try to keep it quick.

438
00:46:24,320 --> 00:46:27,600
When we started making Pok Pok, we started telling people about it.

439
00:46:28,240 --> 00:46:33,680
It was very top secret in our circles, like nobody really knew that we were making it except for journalists in Apple.

440
00:46:34,560 --> 00:46:36,000
And that was very intentional.

441
00:46:37,040 --> 00:46:43,920
We have relationships with journalists from previous jobs, from like our connection with Snowman and so on and so forth.

442
00:46:44,160 --> 00:46:49,760
And we talked to every single journalist who had kids first and said, like, hey, do you want to test this out?

443
00:46:50,240 --> 00:46:55,200
And getting into their hearts and minds early was really important.

444
00:46:55,600 --> 00:46:56,480
We also have a lot of.

445
00:46:57,360 --> 00:47:02,560
If you're in the tech space and you're creating a subscription app, a lot of journalists want to be on that ride.

446
00:47:03,040 --> 00:47:05,760
They want to, they have a lot of opinions about how things should be made.

447
00:47:05,920 --> 00:47:12,480
And so it's pretty awesome to show them an early version of your product and say, hey, give us feedback every now and then, get on the beta.

448
00:47:12,960 --> 00:47:23,840
Sometimes they're on too many and they'll say no, but I think with us, we did it during COVID, especially very, very early days of COVID, like literally the beginning.

449
00:47:24,080 --> 00:47:28,480
So we had a lot of journalists who were like, oh, yes, great, okay, cool, something for my kid to do while I'm working.

450
00:47:29,040 --> 00:47:34,880
So we had a lot of people trying it out and then they also give you valuable feedback.

451
00:47:35,280 --> 00:47:37,200
They start to learn and understand your business.

452
00:47:37,760 --> 00:47:41,360
They get to know you on a personal level, which makes everything way better.

453
00:47:42,080 --> 00:47:45,920
And then they also feel like they're part of the journey with you.

454
00:47:46,240 --> 00:47:48,640
They've given you feedback and they've played with your beta.

455
00:47:49,200 --> 00:47:51,600
They have a connection to this product that they wouldn't have otherwise.

456
00:47:52,240 --> 00:48:07,440
So my suggestion to anyone out there creating an app would be to share it with people as early as you can who will help amplify it when the time comes and I wouldn't be too paranoid about NDAs and all that stuff because no one cares about that.

457
00:48:08,240 --> 00:48:14,320
We were so protective of Pok Pok at the very beginning and then realized like, let's let's share it.

458
00:48:14,480 --> 00:48:15,840
You know, these people are to be trusted.

459
00:48:16,000 --> 00:48:22,960
They're professionals and it's absolutely worth building those relationships because then when you do have news, they're excited.

460
00:48:23,040 --> 00:48:24,560
They're they actually care about it.

461
00:48:24,800 --> 00:48:26,160
It's not like, oh, great, K cool.

462
00:48:26,320 --> 00:48:27,760
So I'm gonna fill my Thursday slot.

463
00:48:27,920 --> 00:48:29,440
They're like, oh damn, that's awesome.

464
00:48:29,600 --> 00:48:30,720
Like, I want to write about this.

465
00:48:30,960 --> 00:48:32,800
And it's more than just a quick upd update.

466
00:48:32,960 --> 00:48:34,160
It's a whole backstory.

467
00:48:34,720 --> 00:48:38,000
And so talking to the press early is really, really important.

468
00:48:38,480 --> 00:48:45,200
And with Apple, um, you know, we just wanted to share what we were making with them and they, they really enjoyed it.

469
00:48:45,360 --> 00:48:48,160
They were like, keep us posted and, and so on and so forth.

470
00:48:48,320 --> 00:48:51,120
And we're really fortunate to have won an Apple Design Award.

471
00:48:51,520 --> 00:48:54,480
And I think just because of that, we, we do get a lot of feature.

472
00:48:54,800 --> 00:48:57,200
Apple likes to highlight Apple Design Award winners.

473
00:48:57,440 --> 00:49:04,560
And that's something we're very proud of and, uh, we're very happy that they, they continue to highlight POCPOC for us.

474
00:49:05,040 --> 00:49:14,160
But the other thing is, the more content you add to your app, the more reason there is to talk about it, whether that's in the press or in the app store or wherever.

475
00:49:14,800 --> 00:49:25,680
So it's important to think about your updates from a content and subscription value point of view, but also from a marketing point of view and really try to balance those narratives.

476
00:49:25,920 --> 00:49:45,440
So for example, we did a big update last year for the Lunar New Year and it was a lot of work internally to make this, as loud an announcement as possible because we it's not just adding content because the lunar year was happening.

477
00:49:45,680 --> 00:49:50,640
It's because we're trying to highlight cultural values and different cultures in POC.

478
00:49:50,720 --> 00:49:50,960
POC.

479
00:49:51,040 --> 00:49:53,440
So that was the first of our cultural updates.

480
00:49:53,680 --> 00:49:57,360
Then we did Pride in June and we have a lot more coming.

481
00:49:57,600 --> 00:50:04,000
And so we're just really adamant about sharing the why's behind what we're making, not just the what's.

482
00:50:04,240 --> 00:50:06,160
And I think that's what journalists tend to care about.

483
00:50:06,320 --> 00:50:10,240
And also, if you're not in the app business, you have no idea who makes apps.

484
00:50:10,560 --> 00:50:13,120
It's like a totally unsexy thing to talk about.

485
00:50:14,000 --> 00:50:19,760
People like, yeah, I have Netflix and like Google Maps on my phone, but nobody cares about that story.

486
00:50:20,080 --> 00:50:21,120
You have to make them care.

487
00:50:21,520 --> 00:50:26,640
So it's really important to us to share who the heck we are and why we're building this.

488
00:50:26,960 --> 00:50:36,960
And Esther and I specifically spend a lot of time just trying to share the fact that like we are real parents, we have real children, we're doing this for a reason.

489
00:50:38,880 --> 00:50:40,240
And people, people are curious.

490
00:50:40,400 --> 00:50:41,280
They want want to know.

491
00:50:41,520 --> 00:50:51,600
And especially if you have a demographic that are millennials or Gen Z, they care so much about who is making their products, whether that's buying a T-shirt or getting a coffee.

492
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:56,320
They want to know if it's ethical, why it was made, how it was made and all that stuff.

493
00:50:56,480 --> 00:51:03,040
So we also work really hard to lift up the curtain and show people who we are and how we make what we're making.

494
00:51:04,640 --> 00:51:12,720
And I guess the last thing I would say on the PR front is it's never too early to start and make sure that you talk to the right people.

495
00:51:13,520 --> 00:51:16,560
Felix can also talk about that because he used to be a journalist in his past life.

496
00:51:17,200 --> 00:51:22,000
But I would say like it's very tough to measure downloads from a news article.

497
00:51:22,560 --> 00:51:23,360
You'll never know.

498
00:51:24,240 --> 00:51:30,960
You can read impressions and all that till the end of the day, but you won't necessarily know, but if you but you'll see it trickle.

499
00:51:31,120 --> 00:51:44,160
You'll see it trickle so many ways in terms of hiring, in terms of raising capital, if you want to raise capital, in terms of just getting the word out there about your brand, because the more people see it and hear about it, the more likely they are to engage with it.

500
00:51:44,560 --> 00:51:50,480
Because in this day and age with social media, you need to see a brand at least ten times before you actually do anything about it.

501
00:51:50,720 --> 00:51:53,280
Like I've been about to buy this shampoo for like three months.

502
00:51:53,680 --> 00:51:54,320
I still do.

503
00:51:55,120 --> 00:51:55,680
And I'm close.

504
00:51:55,840 --> 00:51:56,400
I'm very close.

505
00:51:56,560 --> 00:51:59,280
Yesterday I got to like the end cart thing, but then I aborted.

506
00:51:59,520 --> 00:52:00,320
So maybe tomorrow.

507
00:52:00,960 --> 00:52:02,320
Or you might get a discount then.

508
00:52:03,840 --> 00:52:11,200
But I just wanted to jump in for in terms of a quick advice for app developers when it comes to updates that you do.

509
00:52:11,440 --> 00:52:21,040
It really pays off to do like a great in app event with like a beautiful image and like really figure out what updates make sense for your app.

510
00:52:21,760 --> 00:52:37,920
And it's it's not the longest thing in the world, but like it really goes a long way in getting promoted and getting featured by Apple and putting something really beautiful in front of potential users has been really impactful for us in terms of in app events particularly.

511
00:52:38,880 --> 00:52:38,960
Yeah.

512
00:52:39,280 --> 00:52:41,520
One thing I did want to ask you, Felix, as we wrap up.

513
00:52:42,080 --> 00:52:47,600
So one thing Melissa said there that I think a lot of people listening would be like, oh, she had contacts at Apple.

514
00:52:47,920 --> 00:52:49,680
Oh, she had contacts in the press.

515
00:52:50,160 --> 00:52:51,440
I don't have contacts at Apple.

516
00:52:51,840 --> 00:52:53,040
I don't have contacts in the press.

517
00:52:53,600 --> 00:52:57,200
But the truth is, well, yeah, that's so.

518
00:52:57,280 --> 00:52:59,680
So step one is that, yeah, you have to make them.

519
00:53:00,160 --> 00:53:02,240
But step two is like, how do you make them?

520
00:53:03,120 --> 00:53:09,680
It's that you tell a compelling story and that you weave stories into what you're building.

521
00:53:09,840 --> 00:53:10,880
Like, it has to be interesting.

522
00:53:10,960 --> 00:53:12,560
I mean, I've done this a lot with my apps.

523
00:53:12,800 --> 00:53:20,480
Like, I intentionally build unique, differentiated features into the app and I intentionally use, you know.

524
00:53:20,480 --> 00:53:28,400
use brand new functionality and APIs and use them in ways that people have never used them before because that's an interesting story to tell.

525
00:53:28,960 --> 00:53:52,640
And so what I wanted to ask you, Felix, is that for those who don't have connections, making connections is about telling good stories and journalists, even though, yeah, you may get them to read four more sentences because you have a connection than they would if you send it to the tips email, even if you have a connection, they're not just going to write about you because they know you or whatever.

526
00:53:53,360 --> 00:53:55,680
you still have to have a convincing story to tell.

527
00:53:55,920 --> 00:53:57,280
You still have to build that relationship.

528
00:53:57,600 --> 00:53:58,880
You still have to do all the things.

529
00:53:59,680 --> 00:54:14,000
So for someone who maybe doesn't have those relationships, do you have any tips, Felix, from your experience at Pok Pok and then being a journalist previously and working with other apps of how to approach that storytelling and how to tell convincing stories?

530
00:54:14,640 --> 00:54:14,720
Yeah.

531
00:54:15,520 --> 00:54:21,040
I think the most important part of all this is to be extremely critical of what you're pitching.

532
00:54:21,440 --> 00:54:24,960
Like, you have to assume that journalists are getting a hundred pitches a day.

533
00:54:25,360 --> 00:54:25,440
day.

534
00:54:25,600 --> 00:54:37,440
So like really think about what you're pitching and think about is the journalist really going to like see your thing and be like, oh, that's different from all the other apps I'm getting pitched every day.

535
00:54:37,760 --> 00:54:38,720
Like is it?

536
00:54:39,120 --> 00:54:47,760
Like if it's not rethink what you're doing, like think about your story, like if it's just, oh, like I saw a problem and I I found a solution and here's an app.

537
00:54:48,320 --> 00:54:49,680
Okay, like everyone's saying that.

538
00:54:49,840 --> 00:54:51,280
Like what makes it interesting?

539
00:54:52,160 --> 00:54:54,800
Do you have a unique rapport with this problem?

540
00:54:55,120 --> 00:54:56,880
Like if you're doing it in anxiety.

541
00:54:57,280 --> 00:54:58,880
app, do you suffer from anxiety?

542
00:54:59,040 --> 00:55:00,320
And like, what was the trigger?

543
00:55:00,480 --> 00:55:02,880
Like, you have to find a way to humanize what you're doing.

544
00:55:03,280 --> 00:55:10,720
And also, you have to make sure that there's not 25 apps that are doing exactly the same way and solving the same problem the same way.

545
00:55:11,040 --> 00:55:13,360
Then there's zero chance a journalist is going to talk about it.

546
00:55:13,520 --> 00:55:21,920
So, like, be very critical, think about how you're pitching it to make it like humans like good stories pitch it and make it interesting and get compelling.

547
00:55:22,640 --> 00:55:29,280
And also, uh, make sure you don't copy paste like your messages like journalists can just snap..

548
00:55:29,520 --> 00:55:33,040
So like, try to connect to work they've done before.

549
00:55:33,440 --> 00:55:38,480
Also, make sure you're talking to the right person, because that's all that work you're going to do in advance.

550
00:55:38,800 --> 00:55:48,240
Just make sure you're talking to the right person, that right app thing, not only talks about tech, but has written about apps, has written about your category of apps.

551
00:55:48,640 --> 00:55:50,800
Spending that time will be very rewarding.

552
00:55:51,040 --> 00:55:53,120
And then connect it with stuff that they've done before.

553
00:55:53,520 --> 00:55:56,560
If they talked about kids app, like, oh, that was really interesting.

554
00:55:56,720 --> 00:56:00,000
We're doing something a bit in that field, but we're doing it that way.

555
00:56:00,560 --> 00:56:03,520
And the, the, the, the founder has this crazy story.

556
00:56:03,680 --> 00:56:06,480
So like really think about your pitch, find the right person.

557
00:56:07,360 --> 00:56:16,160
And if you think about it and you don't think you have the right pitch or the right story, that also might be a business model thing that you're not differentiated enough.

558
00:56:16,320 --> 00:56:23,200
So that can help also, uh, thinking back to your business model and like, are we doing something different that people are going to be interested in?

559
00:56:23,600 --> 00:56:27,760
So yeah, that would be my, my advice for people that don't have connection.

560
00:56:28,480 --> 00:56:32,320
Follow journalists on Twitter that you, that are relevant.

561
00:56:32,800 --> 00:56:36,240
Because you'll be surprised how many people actually ask for leads.

562
00:56:36,560 --> 00:56:38,160
So just keep an eye out.

563
00:56:38,560 --> 00:56:40,960
It'll help you understand what they care about, what they write about.

564
